{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FwBbqadp29xRGnDddDNZth","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've now read the full paper and the review context YAML. Let me now dispatch two reviewers in parallel:\n\n- R1: Methods + Experiments\n- R2: Novelty + Related Work\n\nI'll launch them as parallel agents, each doing independent blind review, then I'll conduct the roundtable synthesis.\n\nLet me summarize what I know about the paper first:\n\n**Title:** \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n**Claims:**\n- C1: Method viability — counterfactual pairwise comparison detects formality bias (GPT-4o p=0.006, Claude p=0.037, Gemini p=0.44)\n\n\n- C2: Likert blindspot — Likert scoring is less sensitive than pairwise for detecting style bias\n- C3: Axis applicability — method works for formality but not tone/verbosity (negative controls)\n- C4: Practical guidance — use pairwise, control for original-text preference\n\n**Key results:**\n- Formality bias across judges (Table 1): GPT-4o 69.1%**, Claude 64.7%*, Gemini 56.3% ns\n- Cross-benchmark (Table 2): LLMBar strongest, AlpacaEval marginal, MT-Bench ns\n- Likert vs Pairwise (Table 4): Looking at the comparison metrics between the two evaluation approaches\n\n, Likert never reaches significance while pairwise shows effects in 2 out of 6 cases. The scaled replication with n=500 yields 60.4% with p=0.0008, though there's some regression to the mean. When I control for original preference, the confound is substantial—formal originals get 72.7% preference versus 54.5% for casual ones—but the formality effect itself drops to p=0.11 in logistic regression. Binarizing the Likert scale gives p=0.082, which is ambiguous. The NLI threshold holds up robustly across the 70-95% range, and the consistency filter introduces only mild length selection bias. Human-NLI agreement sits at κ=0.61.\n\nNow I'm checking coverage of the experiments across the paper, from exp-001 through exp-014, and the main formality pairwise work and multi-benchmark analyses are well represented in the tables. I've finished checking the remaining experiments—the human validation and logistic regression analyses are both in the paper. The two deferred experiments were appropriately flagged as limitations, so coverage looks solid. Time to bring in the reviewers.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14395,"cache_read_input_tokens":41309,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14395},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c652419-1a9d-459e-888f-3d09be196f6a","uuid":"8fdf3e2f-5b0a-4187-8943-d0fb8e103f80"}
