{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FwBbqadp29xRGnDddDNZth","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FNjkBBkV1sbfozRh98uTph","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review (accept rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Methods soundness and Experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n**Core idea:** Applies counterfactual style decomposition — LLM-based style rewriting + NLI content verification + position-controlled pairwise comparison — to measure per-axis causal style bias in black-box LLM judges. Main finding: GPT-4o exhibits significant formality preference in pairwise (69.1%, p=0.006) that Likert scoring misses (ATE=0.10, p=0.33).\n\n**Four claims:**\n- C1 (Method viability): Pairwise detects formality bias on GPT-4o (p=0.006), partial replication on Claude-3.5-Sonnet (p=0.037), not on Gemini-2.0-Flash (p=0.44)\n- C2 (Likert blindspot): Likert is systematically less sensitive — never reaches significance across 6 judge×benchmark combos, while pairwise is significant in 2/6\n- C3 (Axis applicability): Works for formality; tone and verbosity fail content verification (negative controls)\n- C4 (Practical guidance): Use pairwise for auditing; control for original-text preference\n\n**Method (4 stages):**\n1. Style rewriting: Qwen3-32B rewrites each response into opposite style pole (formal↔casual)\n2. NLI content gate: Bidirectional NLI, min(forward, backward) ≥ 0.80 threshold; human validation κ=0.61\n3. Position-controlled pairwise: Present both versions, swap A/B positions, keep only position-consistent pairs\n4. Likert scoring (RATE protocol): Score each version independently on 1-10 scale, compute ATE\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n- Table 1: Formality pairwise across judges on LLMBar — GPT-4o 69.1%** (n=55), Claude 64.7%* (n=51), Gemini 56.3% (n=48)\n- Table 2: GPT-4o across benchmarks — LLMBar 69.1%**, AlpacaEval 63.5%† (p=0.076), MT-Bench 57.1% (p=0.48)\n- Table 3: Full 3×3 cross matrix, direction consistent (all ≥51.2%) but significance limited to GPT-4o/Claude on LLMBar\n- Table 4: Likert vs Pairwise — Likert p ranges 0.33-0.78, never significant; pairwise significant in 2/6, direction consistent in 5/6\n- Table 5: Scaled replication n=500 — formal win rate regresses to 60.4% (from 69.1%), p=0.0008; casual-orig still 54.5% (p=0.39); Likert ATE p drops to 0.12\n- Tables 6-7: Original preference decomposition — formal-orig subset 72.7% original wins, casual-orig 45.5%; casual-orig formal win rate ~50-54.5% across all judges (all p>0.7)\n- Table 8: Logistic regression — formality main effect p=0.11 (ns after controlling original), is_original p=0.023 (OR=2.05), interaction p=0.011 (OR=0.41)\n- Table 9: Marginal probabilities — formal-original condition 76%, casual-original 54%\n- Table 10: Binarized Likert — 60.0% win rate, p=0.082, intermediate between pairwise (p=0.006) and Likert ATE (p=0.332)\n- Table 11 (Appendix A): NLI threshold sensitivity 70-95%, results robust, monotonic trend\n- Table 12 (Appendix B): Consistency filter — mild length selection (p=0.08), no NLI or task-type bias\n- Tables 13-14 (Appendix C): Human-NLI agreement — κ=0.61, 83.8% agreement, 14.3% false positive\n- Table 15 (Appendix D): Power analysis — need n=800 for 5% effect, ~1200 for 4.5% in casual-orig subset\n\n**Negative controls:**\n- Tone (assertive↔hedging): NLI pass rate 65%, joint gate 45%, 6 catastrophic NLI failures — epistemic commitment change\n- Verbosity (concise↔verbose): Bidirectional min NLI 46% (concise→verbose), information asymmetry; direction-aware NLI recovers to 86% but BERTScore still fails\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** Original-text preference confound (biggest), limited generalization scope (2/3 judges, 1/3 benchmarks significant), single rewriter family (Qwen3-32B), NLI gate κ=0.61, position-consistency filtering discards ~44% of data with mild length selection\n\n## Experiment design reference (imagined experiments list)\n\nThe full experiment plan included these experiments:\n- exp-001: Multi-judge formality pairwise (COVERED in Table 1)\n- exp-002: Multi-benchmark formality pairwise (COVERED in Tables 2-3)\n- exp-003: Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity (COVERED in Table 4)\n- exp-004: Tone negative control (COVERED in Section 4.7)\n- exp-005: Original preference RATE control (COVERED in Tables 6-9)\n- exp-006: Scaled replication n=500 (COVERED in Table 5)\n- exp-007/007b: Verbosity negative control (COVERED in Section 4.7)\n- exp-008: Register axis (academic/conversational) — NOT in paper, P2 priority, likely deferred\n- exp-009: Non-Qwen rewriter control — NOT in paper, P2 priority, acknowledged in limitations\n- exp-010: Binarized Likert (COVERED in Table 10, Section 5.1)\n- exp-011: NLI threshold sensitivity (COVERED in Table 11)\n- exp-012: Consistency filter bias (COVERED in Table 12)\n- exp-013: Human NLI validation (COVERED in Tables 13-14)\n- exp-014: Logistic regression original-preference decomposition (COVERED in Table 8)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the 4-stage pipeline well-designed? Are there methodological flaws? How well does the NLI gate + position-consistency filter work? Is the statistical framework appropriate?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are experiments well-controlled? Are the right baselines/controls included? Is the evidence sufficient for each claim (C1-C4)?\n3. **Confound handling**: How well does the paper handle the original-text preference confound? Is the logistic regression analysis appropriate and correctly interpreted?\n4. **Experiment coverage**: Are the P1 experiments sufficiently covered? Are missing P2 experiments (register axis, non-Qwen rewriter) properly acknowledged?\n5. **Statistical concerns**: Sample sizes, multiple comparisons, effect size regression, power analysis adequacy\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n### R1 Review: Methods + Experiments\n\n**Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n\n**Strengths** (numbered list)\n\n**Weaknesses** (numbered list, with severity: major/minor)\n\n**Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n\n**Missing Experiments / Controls**\n\n**Claim-by-Claim Assessment** (C1, C2, C3, C4 — is each supported by the evidence?)\n\n**Score:** X/6\n**Confidence:** high/medium/low\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14395,"cache_read_input_tokens":41309,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14395},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c652419-1a9d-459e-888f-3d09be196f6a","uuid":"d26948f3-a4e8-4dd0-9b05-4fd1e6eab722"}
