{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FwBbqadp29xRGnDddDNZth","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01A8hKkQTMHuVeKn9Yx5jGLE","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review (accept rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n**Core idea:** Applies counterfactual style decomposition — combining LLM-based style rewriting, NLI-based content verification, and position-controlled pairwise comparison — to measure per-axis causal style bias in black-box LLM judges. Main finding: GPT-4o exhibits significant formality preference in pairwise comparison (69.1%, p=0.006) that standard Likert scoring fails to detect (ATE=0.10, p=0.33).\n\n**Four claims:**\n- C1 (Method viability): Counterfactual pairwise comparison detects formality bias on GPT-4o (p=0.006), partial replication on Claude-3.5-Sonnet (p=0.037), not significant on Gemini-2.0-Flash (p=0.44)\n- C2 (Likert blindspot): Likert is systematically less sensitive than pairwise — never significant across 6 judge×benchmark combos vs 2/6 significant for pairwise\n- C3 (Axis applicability): Content-preserving counterfactual works for formality; tone (epistemic commitment change) and verbosity (information asymmetry) fail as negative controls\n- C4 (Practical guidance): Prefer pairwise for style bias auditing; control for original-text preference confound\n\n**The paper explicitly acknowledges it combines three known components in a new application:**\n1. LLM-based counterfactual style rewriting (follows RATE, Feuer et al., 2025)\n2. NLI-based content verification (from style transfer evaluation, Mir et al., 2019)\n3. Position-controlled pairwise comparison (standard, Zheng et al., 2023)\n\nThe contribution is claimed as empirical: demonstrating their combination reveals bias invisible to Likert scoring, and characterizing method boundaries.\n\n**Related work cited:**\n- LLM-as-Judge Bias: Zheng et al. 2023 (MT-Bench, position bias), Wang et al. 2023, Park et al. 2024 (length bias), LC-AlpacaEval (Dubois et al. 2024b, length debiasing), SOS-Bench 2025 (superficial features R=0.999), CASSE 2026 (12×12 style interaction matrix, correlational), FairJudge (Lin et al. 2025, demographic fairness)\n- Counterfactual Methods: Veitch et al. 2020, Kaushik et al. 2020, RATE (Feuer et al. 2025, double-rewrite ATE), Counterfactual Rhetorical 2025 (persona-based rhetorical bias)\n- Style Transfer Evaluation: Mir et al. 2019, Krishna et al. 2020, NLI validation from Luo et al. 2019\n- Likert vs Pairwise: Novikova et al. 2018, Kiritchenko and Mohammad 2017\n\n**Key experimental results (for context):**\n- Cross-judge: 2/3 judges significant on LLMBar\n- Cross-benchmark: 1/3 benchmarks significant for GPT-4o, AlpacaEval marginal\n- Full 3×3 matrix: direction consistent but significance sparse\n- Original-text preference confound: formality main effect becomes non-significant (p=0.11) after controlling for original-text preference; interaction significant (p=0.011)\n- Binarized Likert: p=0.082, ambiguous — doesn't fully resolve whether blindspot is structural or statistical\n- Negative controls (tone, verbosity) establish method boundaries\n\n**The paper is 8 pages main text + 3 pages appendix + references.**\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: The paper self-describes as combining known components. Is the empirical contribution (revealing Likert blindspot + characterizing method boundaries) sufficiently novel for a top venue? What is genuinely new here vs. incremental application?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines/comparisons missing? How does this position against RATE (Feuer et al. 2025), CASSE 2026, SOS-Bench 2025, Counterfactual Rhetorical 2025? Are there papers on Likert vs pairwise comparison in NLG evaluation that should be cited?\n3. **Differentiation from RATE**: The paper uses RATE's double-rewrite protocol in Stage 4 and follows their counterfactual rewriting approach. How clearly does this paper differentiate its contribution from RATE?\n4. **Scope of contribution vs. claims**: Are the claims appropriately scoped given the evidence? The paper has honest hedging but is it enough?\n5. **Significance and impact**: Would this change how people do LLM evaluation? Is the Likert blindspot finding actionable?\n6. **Writing quality**: Is the paper well-written, well-organized, appropriately concise?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n### R2 Review: Novelty + Related Work\n\n**Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n\n**Strengths** (numbered list)\n\n**Weaknesses** (numbered list, with severity: major/minor)\n\n**Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n\n**Novelty Assessment** (detailed paragraph)\n\n**Related Work Gaps**\n\n**Significance / Impact Assessment**\n\n**Score:** X/6\n**Confidence:** high/medium/low\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14395,"cache_read_input_tokens":41309,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14395},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c652419-1a9d-459e-888f-3d09be196f6a","uuid":"1958d102-b540-41f3-930a-f77d558ca602"}
