{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019HyS397MCSTjtUK7T6ArDk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260520T233719\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 4/6 Weak Accept — borderline Main, strong Findings\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-21T00:23:20.463595+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: \"The Likert Blindspot\"\n9\t\n10\t**dream_id:** 20260520T233719 | **review_mode:** full | **date:** 2026-05-21\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## Paper Summary\n15\t\n16\tThe paper proposes Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD), a pipeline combining LLM-based style rewriting, NLI content verification, and position-controlled pairwise comparison to detect per-axis style bias in black-box LLM judges. The central empirical finding (\"Likert Blindspot\") is that standard Likert pointwise scoring fails to detect formality bias (0/9 judge×benchmark combinations significant) while pairwise comparison on the same counterfactual data detects it reliably (7/9 significant, mean win rate 66.4%). A binarized Likert control isolates this as a measurement granularity problem rather than a statistical test artifact. A logistic regression decomposes formality from original-preference confounds (OR=1.68, p=0.006). Register replicates the pattern; tone fails the NLI gate, providing a principled method boundary. 9 pages, ~25 references.\n17\t\n18\t---\n19\t\n20\t## Reviewer Panel\n21\t\n22\t- **R1** — Methods & Experimental Rigor\n23\t- **R2** — Novelty, Positioning & Related Work\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t## Consensus Strengths\n28\t\n29\t**S1. The Likert Blindspot is well-isolated and practically important.** The 0/9 vs 7/9 contrast across a full 3×3 matrix is striking. The 30× median p-value gap (0.39 vs 0.012) is hard to dismiss. No reverse pattern exists (0/9 cases where Likert detects bias that pairwise misses). Both reviewers flagged this as the strongest contribution. [R1, R2]\n30\t\n31\t**S2. The binarized Likert control (Table 3) is a clever experimental design.** By applying the same sign test to binarized Likert scores, the paper cleanly separates the measurement-structure explanation from the test-choice explanation. This is the paper's most methodologically sophisticated contribution. [R1, R2]\n32\t\n33\t**S3. Unusually thorough robustness battery.** 13 analyses covering: n=500 replication, NLI threshold sensitivity, rewriter ablation (Qwen3 vs Llama), consistency filter bias, human NLI validation (κ=0.72), original preference control (RATE), and logistic regression decomposition. The n=500 replication where Likert remains non-significant (p=0.082) while pairwise reaches p<0.001 effectively kills the power-deficit counterargument. [R1, R2]\n34\t\n35\t**S4. Honest contribution framing.** C1 explicitly acknowledged as application (combining known components), C3 as preliminary. This calibration builds trust. [R2]\n36\t\n37\t**S5. Principled method boundary from negative control.** The tone axis failure, analyzed mechanistically (epistemic commitment changes violate NLI, 30% catastrophic failures), is more informative than a third positive result. Gives practitioners a clear decision criterion. [R1, R2]\n38\t\n39\t---\n40\t\n41\t## Consensus Weaknesses\n42\t\n43\t### Major\n44\t\n45\t**W1. Binarized Likert control shown for only 1/9 combinations (GPT-4o×LLMBar).** [R1]\n46\tThis is the paper's most decisive evidence for C2, yet it appears only for a single cell of the 3×3 matrix. Gemini×LLMBar (72.4% pairwise win rate) is the strongest-effect combination — if binarized Likert *also* reaches significance there, the \"structural measurement limitation\" claim weakens. The paper must show this control for at minimum the 3 LLMBar combinations (all judges), ideally all 9. This is a zero-cost reanalysis of existing data.\n47\t**Severity: Major. Actionable: yes, zero-cost.**\n48\t\n49\t**W2. \"Causal\" language overclaimed.** [R2]\n50\tThe paper uses \"causal\" extensively (method name \"Counterfactual Style Decomposition,\" \"independent causal effect,\" \"causal factor\"). However: no DAG, no do-calculus, no SUTVA discussion, no sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding. The logistic regression (Table 8) controls for measured confounds but is associational, not causal. The counterfactual rewriting + NLI verification design provides strong *interventional* evidence, but formal causal identification requires more scaffolding. Should either (a) provide a minimal DAG with stated assumptions, or (b) soften to \"interventional\" / \"quasi-experimental.\"\n51\t**Severity: Major. Actionable: yes, framing change.**\n52\t\n53\t**W3. Known psychometric limitation being confirmed — novelty debatable.** [R2]\n54\tJamieson (2004), Thurstone (1927), and Karpinska et al. (2021) — all cited — already document Likert insensitivity and forced-choice superiority. The mechanism (measurement granularity, tied scores, scale resolution) was predicted by existing theory. The contribution is confirming this in the LLM-judge-style-bias setting with specific quantification. The paper should more forthrightly frame the novelty as *empirical demonstration in a high-stakes applied context + quantification of the gap magnitude*, rather than presenting the Likert Blindspot as a discovery.\n55\t**Severity: Major. Actionable: yes, framing change.**\n56\t\n57\t**W4. Logistic regression ignores within-pair dependence.** [R1]\n58\tTable 8's logistic regression pools N=459 observations from pairs evaluated in both directions, but does not account for within-pair clustering. The original preference coefficient (p=0.046) is borderline — under GEE or clustered standard errors, it may flip to non-significant, which would change the decomposition narrative. The paper should report both naive and clustered results.\n59\t**Severity: Major. Actionable: yes, zero-cost reanalysis.**\n60\t\n61\t**W5. Insufficient differentiation from RATE [Feuer et al. 2025] and Zhou et al. [2025].** [R2]\n62\tRATE already provides the double-rewrite ATE framework. Zhou et al. already applied counterfactual evaluation to LLM judges. The related work gives Zhou et al. a single sentence. The paper's delta is: (a) applying to style axes, and (b) the Likert-vs-pairwise comparison. Only (b) is substantively new. Needs a clearer articulation (comparison table or paragraph) of what each prior method can/cannot do.\n63\t**Severity: Major. Actionable: yes, writing change.**\n64\t\n65\t**W6. No alternative pointwise scales tested.** [R1]\n66\tThe paper tests only a discrete 1–10 Likert scale. Without testing continuous scales (1–100, slider, log-probability), we cannot determine whether the blindspot is specific to coarse discrete scales or inherent to all absolute scoring. This limits the generalizability of C2's core claim (\"Likert scoring structurally underestimates\").\n67\t**Severity: Major. Harder to fix (requires new experiments).**\n68\t\n69\t### Minor\n70\t\n71\t**W7. Placeholder references.** [R2]\n72\tChen et al. [2026] (\"2601.xxxxx\"), Lu et al. [2025] CASSE (\"2501.xxxxx\"), Saito et al. [2023] (\"2310.xxxxx\"), Zhou et al. [2025] (\"2502.xxxxx\") all have placeholder arXiv IDs. If these are real papers, actual IDs must be provided. If fabricated, this is a serious integrity issue.\n73\t**Severity: Minor (fixable), but potentially escalates if references are not real.**\n74\t\n75\t**W8. 44% discard rate from position-consistency filtering.** [R1, R2]\n76\tWhile no systematic bias is demonstrated in retained vs. discarded pairs (5 tests, all p>0.38), discarding nearly half the data means CSD characterizes bias only for the subset of inputs where judges are position-consistent. This may exclude precisely the contested cases where style bias operates most. Deserves more discussion.\n77\t**Severity: Minor.**\n78\t\n79\t**W9. SOS-Bench positioning slightly unfair.** [R2]\n80\tThe paper frames SOS-Bench as \"correlational,\" which understates their injection methodology (controlled defect injection with 152K data points is itself interventional). Should acknowledge SOS-Bench's strengths more fairly.\n81\t**Severity: Minor.**\n82\t\n83\t**W10. Modest effect size with thin practical guidance.** [R2]\n84\tCSD-adjusted formality bias is ~13pp. The practical correction mechanism (subtract original preference estimate) is rudimentary compared to LC-AlpacaEval's regression framework. C4 amounts to four bullet points.\n85\t**Severity: Minor.**\n86\t\n87\t---\n88\t\n89\t## Questions for Authors\n90\t\n91\t1. Can you provide actual arXiv IDs for all four placeholder references? (W7)\n92\t2. What does the binarized Likert control look like for Gemini×LLMBar (strongest effect)? If binarized Likert is significant there, how does that affect the C2 claim? (W1)\n93\t3. Given McFadden R²=0.042, how should readers interpret the significant formality OR? The model explains very little variance. (W4 related)\n94\t4. The direction asymmetry (formal→original 78.8% vs casual→original 54.5% in MVP) is substantial. Does this suggest the \"formality bias\" partly reflects rewriting-quality asymmetry? (R2)\n95\t5. Have you considered GEE or mixed-effects logistic regression to account for within-pair clustering? (W4)\n96\t6. Would the Likert Blindspot persist with a 1–100 continuous scale or with judge log-probabilities? (W6)\n97\t\n98\t---\n99\t\n100\t## Missing References (R2)\n101\t\n102\t- Ye et al. (2024), \"FLASK\" — multi-dimensional LLM evaluation\n103\t- Stureborg et al. (2024), \"Large Language Models are Inconsistent and Biased Evaluators\"\n104\t- Chiang & Lee (2023), \"Can Large Language Models Be an Alternative to Human Evaluations?\"\n105\t- Li et al. (2024), \"Split and Merge\" — position bias mitigation\n106\t\n107\t---\n108\t\n109\t## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n110\t\n111\tThe review_context.yaml lists 13 imagined experiments. The paper covers all 13:\n112\t- exp-001 through exp-006, exp-008 through exp-014: all present in tables or appendix\n113\t- Coverage: **13/13 (100%)**. Excellent.\n114\t\n115\tThe paper's experimental program is comprehensive and well-organized. The main gap is not missing experiments but missing *analyses on existing data* (binarized Likert across full matrix, GEE correction).\n116\t\n117\t---\n118\t\n119\t## Scores\n120\t\n121\t| Reviewer | Score | Confidence |\n122\t|----------|-------|------------|\n123\t| R1 (Methods) | 4/6 (Weak Accept) | 4/5 |\n124\t| R2 (Novelty) | 4/6 (Weak Accept) | 4/5 |\n125\t| **Consolidated** | **4/6 (Weak Accept)** | **4/5** |\n126\t\n127\t---\n128\t\n129\t## Meta-Assessment\n130\t\n131\t**Borderline Main / strong Findings.** The paper presents a well-executed empirical study with a practically important finding (Likert scoring misses style bias) backed by thorough robustness checks and 100% experiment coverage. The binarized Likert control is clever and the 3×3 matrix provides convincing cross-validation.\n132\t\n133\tHowever, three structural issues hold it at the Main threshold boundary:\n134\t1. The core finding confirms a known psychometric principle in a new domain — valuable but incremental\n135\t2. The decisive binarized Likert evidence appears for only 1/9 combinations\n136\t3. Causal language exceeds the formal identification support\n137\t\n138\t**Clear path to 5/6 (Accept):** Extend binarized Likert to all 9 combinations, report GEE-corrected logistic regression, soften causal language to \"interventional,\" and more explicitly differentiate from RATE/Zhou. All are low-cost changes on existing data. If the binarized Likert pattern holds across all 9 combinations — which the evidence suggests it will — the C2 claim becomes substantially stronger.\n139\t\n140\t**Risk factor:** If binarized Likert reaches significance for Gemini (strongest effect at 72.4%), the \"structural limitation\" narrative weakens to \"scale-dependent limitation,\" which is still interesting but narrower than claimed.\n141\t\n142\t**Overall recommendation: Revise and resubmit (lean accept).** The core insight is sound, the execution is thorough, and the fixes are straightforward. With the identified revisions, this paper should clear the Main conference bar."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A8hKkQTMHuVeKn9Yx5jGLE","session_id":"5c652419-1a9d-459e-888f-3d09be196f6a","uuid":"b01ac5be-46ec-4900-b582-a6b8ef5afcf9","timestamp":"2026-05-21T00:25:48.449Z"}
